**Related Spec**: specs/438-return-write-spec-to-native-plan-mode-after-merged-publication/

# File: hooks/register.test.ts, scripts/__tests__/sdlc-commands.test.mjs, scripts/__tests__/publish-approved-spec.test.mjs
# Generated from: specs/489-fix-write-spec-stopping-silently-after-a-spec-merge-that-outlasts-the-bash-timeout/requirements.md
Feature: Write-spec continues after a spec merge that outlasts the Bash timeout

  @SCN001 @regression
  Scenario: A merged publication is recognized from the helper result
    Given a write-spec session
    And a Bash command invoking publish-approved-spec.mjs merge --issue 489 with single quotes and a trailing-slash plugin root
    When the call returns a sole JSON object with merged true, pr 490, issue 489, and dir "specs/489-slug"
    Then published[] contains issue 489 with slug "slug" exactly once
    And the continuation is pending

  @SCN002 @regression
  Scenario: The merge helper reports the published issue and directory
    Given an approved spec package for issue 42 on branch 42-add-x
    When publish-approved-spec.mjs merge --issue 42 --dir specs/42-add-x merges the PR, including when checkout or labeling fails afterward
    Then its JSON result has merged true, issue 42, and dir "specs/42-add-x"

  @SCN003 @regression
  Scenario: The session returns to the picker in plan mode
    Given a recognized merged publication for issue 489
    When the main-session turn ends with an empty prompt box
    Then the hooks module runs /plan
    And submits one continuation prompt listing issue 489

  @SCN004 @regression
  Scenario: An unsubmitted continuation stays pending and is retried
    Given a recognized merged publication for issue 489
    And the prompt box holds a draft
    When the main-session turn ends
    Then a toast names "#489" and says the Continue picker opens after the next turn that ends with an empty prompt box
    And no continuation is submitted
    When a later main-session turn ends with an empty prompt box
    Then the continuation prompt is submitted into plan mode

  @SCN005 @regression
  Scenario: Non-publication Bash calls are not treated as publications
    Given a write-spec session with no backgrounded merge outstanding
    When an unrelated Bash call returns merged JSON, or a merge call returns several JSON objects, no merged flag, or an issue that disagrees with dir or --issue
    Then published[] is unchanged
    And no continuation is submitted when the turn ends

  @SCN006 @regression
  Scenario: Existing Finished and explicit-number behavior is preserved
    Given a write-spec session started with /sdlc-write-spec #489
    When its publication merges and the person later selects "Finished — stop writing specs"
    Then a continuation was submitted after the publication
    And the reply's ExitPlanMode is approved without a dialog

  @SCN007 @regression
  Scenario: The loop continues until Finished
    Given a write-spec session that published issue 489
    When issue 491's publication merges and the main-session turn ends with an empty prompt box
    Then a continuation prompt listing issues 489 and 491 is submitted into plan mode
    And after a Finished answer no further continuation is submitted

  @SCN008 @regression
  Scenario: A background merge is recognized when it completes
    Given a write-spec merge Bash call for issue 489 run with run_in_background true
    And its result has a backgroundTaskId and empty stdout
    When a later Bash cat call returns the sole merged JSON with issue 489 and dir "specs/489-slug"
    And the main-session turn ends with an empty prompt box
    Then published[] contains issue 489 exactly once
    And the continuation prompt is submitted into plan mode

  @SCN009 @regression
  Scenario: The workflow waits for CI success or failure without a timeout
    Given the rendered write-spec workflow
    When its Approval Behavior merge step is read
    Then it runs publish-approved-spec.mjs merge with run_in_background true
    And it waits for the task's completion notification with no timeout or stall rule before reading the complete output
